{
  email ops@sparx.works
  admin off

  # On-demand TLS — issues Let's Encrypt certs for tenant subdomains
  # (slug.sparx.zone) and custom domains (shop.merchant.com) on first
  # HTTPS request. Validated against the API before issuance to prevent
  # unauthorized cert minting.
  on_demand_tls {
    ask http://api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000/internal/domain-check
    interval 2m
    burst 5
  }
}

# Baseline security headers (docs/50 §4). Conservative on purpose: HSTS
# (no includeSubDomains/preload, to avoid surprising tenant custom domains),
# nosniff, clickjacking protection, and a tight referrer policy. Deliberately
# NO Content-Security-Policy or Permissions-Policy here — those need per-app
# tuning (Stripe/checkout, PostHog, Satori) and land as a Report-Only pass.
(security_headers) {
  header {
    Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
    X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
    Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
  }
}

# ----- HTTP → HTTPS -----
:80 {
  redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}

# ----- Platform hostnames (Cloudflare-proxied; cert from on-demand) -----
api.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# media.sparx.works — public CDN domain for transcoded media variants.
# Cloudflare caches everything for a year; the origin only sees a sliver
# of traffic, and merchants get fast TTFB worldwide. The path under it
# is identical to api.sparx.works so MEDIA_PUBLIC_URL is a single env
# swap. Only the /v1/public/media/* routes serve here — anything else
# 404s by virtue of the api-rest route map.
media.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  header {
    # Strip any cookies merchant proxies might forward — the CDN
    # variants are pure content.
    -Set-Cookie
  }
  # NOTE: do NOT stamp a blanket `Cache-Control: immutable` here. api-rest
  # owns cache semantics per-path — immutable on variant bytes, 86400 on the
  # resolver redirect, and a deliberate 60s while an image is still
  # transcoding (services/api-rest/src/routes/v1/public/media.ts). A blanket
  # header overrode that 60s window (pinning a mid-transcode original for a
  # year) AND — worse — cached 404/422 ERROR responses for a year, so a
  # variant that wasn't ready at first fetch stayed permanently broken at the
  # Cloudflare edge. The origin app sets the right header on every success
  # path, so it survives a CF bypass without this.
  reverse_proxy api-rest.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

graphql.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy api-graphql.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3200
}

mcp.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy api-mcp.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# Canonical shopper-facing site MCP host (docs/113). A reserved slug, so
# no tenant owns it; authorized as a static platform host in
# /internal/domain-check. The store is named in the path
# (/s/<tenant>[/<property>]/mcp) since a fixed host can't encode it.
mcp.sparx.zone {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy mcp-site.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3200
}

# The staff app. Served by the workbench (apps/workbench), which replaced the
# decommissioned dashboard here. app.sparx.works is also the MCP OAuth
# authorization-server origin (MCP_AUTH_SERVER_URL) and the Better Auth origin —
# the workbench serves the /.well-known discovery docs + /oauth/consent.
app.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy workbench.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# Legacy host from when the workbench ran beside the dashboard. It now lives at
# app.sparx.works (the canonical staff URL), so send this subdomain there — a
# permanent redirect keeps any old bookmark/link working. Better Auth cookies +
# the OAuth origin are scoped to app.sparx.works, so this host must NOT serve the
# app itself.
workbench.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  redir https://app.sparx.works{uri} permanent
}

sparx.works, www.sparx.works {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers

  # Social-card images are proxied with response BUFFERING (`flush_interval -1`)
  # so Caddy emits a real Content-Length instead of streaming them back chunked.
  #
  # Why this is needed: LinkedIn's image fetcher sizes an image from
  # Content-Length before it will download it. Without the header it reports
  # "cannot find an image" even though the URL returns 200 image/png and the
  # og:image tags are complete — which is exactly the failure we hit. A HEAD on
  # these paths was returning neither Content-Length nor Transfer-Encoding.
  # Browsers, Slack, and X all tolerate the chunked response, so this presented
  # as LinkedIn-only.
  #
  # Deliberately scoped to the two Next metadata-image conventions rather than
  # applied to the whole site: `flush_interval -1` disables streaming, which
  # would break Server-Sent Events / token streaming used by the chat widget.
  # These cards are small (~60KB) and fully rendered before send, so buffering
  # them costs nothing.
  @social path */opengraph-image */twitter-image
  reverse_proxy @social web.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000 {
    flush_interval -1
  }

  reverse_proxy web.sparx-prod.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

# ----- kanNINJA (co-tenant in the `kanninja` namespace) -----
# Added 2026-06-06 during the kanNINJA AKS→GKE migration. kanNINJA's workloads
# live in their own `kanninja` namespace; Caddy reaches them cross-namespace via
# ClusterIP Services (port 80 → app's http port).
#
# IMPORTANT: these blocks deliberately OMIT `tls { on_demand }`. On-demand would
# call the global ask endpoint (api-rest /internal/domain-check), which only
# knows sparx tenants/custom domains and would REJECT kanninja.com — no cert.
# Explicit host blocks get a normal Caddy-managed Let's Encrypt cert (HTTP-01 /
# TLS-ALPN), which is what we want. Requires kanninja.com / api / mcp DNS to
# resolve to this LB (35.254.145.54) first; keep them DNS-only (no Cloudflare
# proxy) until the certs issue, then optionally proxy with Full(strict).
kanninja.com {
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy frontend.kanninja.svc.cluster.local:80
}

www.kanninja.com {
  import security_headers
  redir https://kanninja.com{uri} permanent
}

api.kanninja.com {
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy backend.kanninja.svc.cluster.local:80
}

mcp.kanninja.com {
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy mcp.kanninja.svc.cluster.local:80
}

# ----- SilicaUI marketing + docs site (co-tenant in the `silicaui` namespace) -----
# The site's workloads live in their own `silicaui` namespace (a static Next.js
# export served by nginx), reached cross-namespace via its ClusterIP Service.
#
# Uses `tls { on_demand }` (like the sparx platform hosts, NOT the kanNINJA-style
# explicit managed block). silicaui.com/www are allow-listed as static platform
# hosts in the on-demand ask endpoint (api-rest /internal/domain-check
# PLATFORM_HOSTNAMES), so issuance is authorized. On-demand is deliberate here:
# this Caddy is a single replica on a Recreate rollout, so every boot has a
# ~15-60s window with no registered LB backend, and an explicit block fires all
# its startup issuance attempts inside it — a first cert can never be obtained at
# boot (this is exactly what blocked silicaui's cert). On-demand defers issuance
# to the first HTTPS request, which lands on the warm, LB-registered pod, so the
# ACME challenge succeeds. Requires silicaui.com / www DNS to resolve to this LB
# (35.254.145.54) first.
silicaui.com {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  reverse_proxy site.silicaui.svc.cluster.local:80
}

www.silicaui.com {
  tls {
    on_demand
  }
  import security_headers
  redir https://silicaui.com{uri} permanent
}

# ----- WizeWorks operator console (admin.wize.works, wize-admin namespace) -----
# The Layer-4 operator console (docs/16 §2.4, docs/apps/admin/build-plan.md).
# Access control is enforced IN FRONT by Cloudflare Access (D8/D9), the
# load-bearing gate until operator MFA ships; Caddy just proxies cross-namespace
# to the admin Service.
#
# TLS is a Cloudflare ORIGIN CA cert, NOT Let's Encrypt. This host sits behind
# Cloudflare Access, which intercepts the ACME challenge path (/.well-known/
# acme-challenge/*) and returns its login page — so Caddy can NEVER obtain an LE
# cert while proxied (both tls-alpn-01 and http-01 fail). The Origin CA cert is
# signed by Cloudflare (trusted by its edge in Full(strict)), valid 15 years, and
# never renews. Generated + stored by terraform (origin-ca.tf); synced into the
# caddy-admin-origin secret + mounted at /certs/admin by bootstrap.yml
# (components=caddy). The record stays proxied=true — no Terraform drift.
#
# NOTE: this `tls` directive makes the whole shared ingress depend on the cert
# files existing. The bootstrap caddy step syncs the secret and FAILS before
# rolling if it can't — do not remove that guard.
admin.wize.works {
  import security_headers
  tls /certs/admin/tls.crt /certs/admin/tls.key
  reverse_proxy admin.wize-admin.svc.cluster.local:3000
}

